*******************************************************************************
                      What Is New in PCBoard 15.4
              (Unreleased beta — recovered by pcbirc crew 2026)
*******************************************************************************

NOTE: PCBoard 15.4 was never officially released by Clark Development
Company. These changes were found in the 15.4b beta source code and
MKPCBTXT.EXE binary. This document reconstructs what Clark intended
for 15.4 based on source code analysis.


PCBoard Additions/Changes
=========================

Personal Storage Area (PSA_PERSONAL)
-------------------------------------
Added user profile fields as a new Personal Storage Area (PSA):
  - Gender (M/F)
  - Birthdate
  - WEB address (URL)

These fields are stored in the user record as PSA_PERSONAL, a new
entry in PCBoard's modular PSA system (PSA_ALIAS, PSA_VERIFY,
PSA_ADDRESS, PSA_PASSWORD, PSA_STATS, PSA_NOTES, PSA_ACCOUNT,
PSA_QWKNET, PSA_PERSONAL, PSA_BANK). Prompted during new user
registration or profile update.


PCBTEXT String Additions
------------------------
Four new PCBTEXT strings added (slots 747-750):

  747: "Enter your gender (M/F)"
  748: "Enter your birthdate"
  749: "Enter your WEB address"
  750: "Enter color (B)lue,(G)reen,(C)yan,(R)ed,(M)agenta,(Y)ellow,(W)hite,(+/-)"

The version tag in STRS15.C for these strings is 154, confirming they
were intended for PCBoard 15.4.

Sysops should run MKPCBTXT to regenerate their PCBTEXT file.


PCBOARD.DAT
------------
Line 349 remains the last field (same as 15.3). No new PCBOARD.DAT
lines were added in 15.4.


Source Code Changes from 15.3
-----------------------------
The 15.3 source (pcb153src0014) contains 14 internal snapshots
(versions 000 through 014) showing incremental development. Changes
include bug fixes, code cleanup, and preparation for the Personal Storage Area
(PSA_PERSONAL) fields. The PCBSRCV directory preserves all 14 snapshots.


Compiler
--------
Clark's 15.3/15.4 used Borland C++ 3.1 with Borland inline assembler.
The pcbirc project has ported the codebase to OpenWatcom 2.0 for
cross-platform compilation — see PCB1541.NEW for details.
